          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
              \-calculus
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computational"} computational
            model for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concurrency"} concurrent processes
            that communicate messages through named channels. It subsumes
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%CE%BB-calculus"} λ-calculus
          %p
            In the
            %em monadic
            (here meaning:
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unary"} unary
            = succeed "-calculus," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            %em polyadic
            (here meaning:
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-ary"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                  \-ary
            = succeed "-calculus" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
          %p
            This calculus permits non-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confluent"} confluent
            reductions by design, as opposed to other process calculi such as the Interactive Lambda Calculus (ILC).
          %h2#syntax Syntax
          %p A process is either:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                an inactive process
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            %li
              %p
                the parallel composition of two processes
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                        %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                        %mi Q
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P\mid Q
            %li
              %p
                the replication of a process
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo !
                        %mi P
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !P
            %li
              %p
                a restriction
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ν
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\nu n)\,P
                (that is the generation of a fresh name only known to
                = succeed ");" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            %li
              %p
                prefixed by an input
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi m
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo .
                        %mi P
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n(m).P
            %li
              %p
                prefixed by an output
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi n
                          %mo ¯
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                        %mi m
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                        %mo .
                        %mi P
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{n}\langle m\rangle.P
          %p
            Sometimes an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nondeterministic+computation"} indeterministic
            choice operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} +
            is added to the syntax.
          %p
            The central
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inference+rule"} reduction rule
            of the
            = succeed "-calculus" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi n
                    %mo ¯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo .
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                  %mi Q
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ↦
                      %mi b
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\overline{n}\langle a\rangle.P\mid n(b).Q
                  \\;\longrightarrow\;
                  P\mid Q_{a\mapsto b}
                  \\,.
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  a server that increments the value it receives:
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi S
                          %mo =
                          %mo !
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} incr
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi a
                          %mo ,
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo .
                          %mover
                            %mi a
                            %mo ¯
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                          %mi x
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S=!\mathrm{incr}(a,x).\overline{a}\langle x+1\rangle
              %li
                %p
                  a client that calls to the
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} incr
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{incr}
                  server:
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi C
                          %mo =
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi ν
                          %mi a
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                          %mover
                            %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} incr
                            %mo ¯
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                          %mi a
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 4
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                          %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                          %mi a
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C=(\nu a)\bigl(\overline{\mathrm{incr}}\langle a,4\rangle\mid a(y)\bigr)
              %li
                %p
                  the final process by composing the server and the client:
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi S
                          %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                          %mi C
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S\mid C
            %p
          %h2#typed_calculus
            Typed
            = succeed "-calculus" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
          %p
            Several attempts have been made to give the
            = succeed "-calculus" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/typing+systems"} typing systems
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} HO
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{HO}\pi
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher-order+logic"} higher-order
            = succeed "-calculus" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
          %h3#simple_types Simple types
          %p Simple types are defined by the following grammar:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mo ♯
                  %mover
                    %mi T
                    %mo ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T\longrightarrow\sharp\widetilde{T}
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi T
                    %mo ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widetilde{T}
            ranges over (possibly empty) tuples of simple types.
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} Typing contexts
            are lists of hypotheses of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \colon T
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            is a channel name and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is a simple type.
          %p
            Simply typed
            = succeed "-calculus" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            = succeed ":" do
              %span.newWikiWord
                distributed logic
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/distributed+logic"} ?
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th Typing
                %th Intuitive signification
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo ,
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo .
                        %mi P
                        %mo :
                        %mi p
                        %mo ,
                        %mi q
                        %mo →
                        %mi r
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a(x,y).P:p,q\to r
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %strong Lemma 1.
                  Under hypotheses
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi p
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi q
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi r
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi a
                          %mo ¯
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                        %mi M
                        %mo ,
                        %mi N
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                        %mo :
                        %mi r
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{a}\langle M,N\rangle:r
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Use Lemma 1, knowing that
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
                  and
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi q
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi r
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
          %h3#session_types Session types
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/session+type"} Session types
            were introduced by Kohei Honda. A session links exactly two subprocesses that interact with each other, and multiple sessions can run simultaneously.
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              The process
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo .
                    %mover
                      %mi x
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mn .0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x(n).\overline{x}\langle n+1\rangle.0
              may be given type
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mo ?
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                        %mi int
                      %mo .
                      %mo !
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                        %mi int
                      %mo .
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                        %mi end
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x:?\mathsf{int}.!\mathsf{int}.\mathsf{end}
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Caires2010"} Caires and Pfenning 2010
            established a correspondence between
            %span.newWikiWord
              dual intuitionistic
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/dual+intuitionistic+linear+logic"} ?
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            (DILL) and session typed
            = succeed "-calculus." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            %a{:href => "#Walder2012"} Wadler 2012
            established a correspondence between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+linear+logic"} classical linear logic
            (CLL) and the same calculus.
          %h2#other_process_calculi Other process calculi
          %p
            The Calculus of Communicating Systems (CCS) is an important predecessor of the π-calculus. It can be seen as a restriction of π in which only a dummy name
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi w
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w
            is exchanged in communications, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi w
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w
            cannot be used in subject position (
            %a{:href => "#Hirschkoff03"}> Hirschkoff 2003
            ).
          %h3#the_applied_calculus The applied π-calculus
          %p
            The
            %em applied π-calculus
            is an extension of the π-calculus with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"} terms
            (representing messages in security protocols) instead of names. In addition, function symbols represent cryptographic primitives and are related by an equational
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robin+Milner"}> Robin Milner
                , Joachim Parrow and David Walker,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A Calculus of Mobile Processes
                %strong 100
                (1992) 1-40.
            %li
              %p
                Peter Sewell,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Applied
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi π
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
                    – A Brief Tutorial
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.cl.cam.ac.uk/~pes20/apppi.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                Jeannette M. Wing,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    FAQ on
                    = succeed "-Calculus" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi π
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~wing/publications/Wing02a.pdf"} pdf
            %li#Hirschkoff03
              %p
                Daniel Hirschkoff,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    A brief survey of the theory of the
                    = succeed "-calculus" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi π
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "https://hal-lara.archives-ouvertes.fr/hal-02101985/file/RR2003-13.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pierre-Louis+Curien"}> Pierre-Louis Curien
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Introduction to CCS and
                    = succeed "-calculus" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi pi
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} pi
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.irif.fr/~curien/CCS-DEA.ps.gz"} ps
          %p
            With
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Kohei Honda,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Types for dyadic interaction
            %li
              %p
                Noël Bernard,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Investigating the Logical Aspects of the Pi-calculus
                %strong 12
                (2003) 57-66 (
                %a{:href => "https://hal.science/hal-00386109"}> hal-00386109
                ).
            %li#Caires10
              %p
                Luís Caires and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frank+Pfenning"} Frank Pfenning
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Session Types as Intuitionistic Linear Propositions
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~fp/papers/concur10.pdf"} pdf
            %li#Walder2012
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Philip+Wadler"}> Philip Wadler
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Propositions as Sessions
              %p
                ACM SIGPLAN Notices
                %strong 47
                9 (2012) 273 - 286 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/2398856.2364568"}> doi:10.1145/2398856.2364568
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://homepages.inf.ed.ac.uk/wadler/papers/propositions-as-sessions/propositions-as-sessions.pdf"} pdf
              %p
                Journal of Functional Programming
                %strong 24
                2-3 ICFP 2012 (2014) 384-418 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/S095679681400001X"}> doi:10.1017/S095679681400001X
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p Other process calculi:
          %ul
            %li
              Kevin Liao, Matthew A. Hammer and Andrew Miller,
              = succeed "," do
                %em ILC: A Calculus for Composable, Computational Cryptography
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3314221.3314607"} doi:10.1145/3314221.3314607
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/402.pdf"} pdf
